<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/"
      type="topic" id="printer-status">
  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#printer"/>
    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <link type="seealso" xref="printing"/>
    <desc>Find out why a label did not print: the printer's faults, what it
    is doing, its memory and its wear, and a console for anything
    else.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Printer status and the console</title>

  <section id="status">
    <title>Printer status</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Printer</gui><gui>Status…</gui></guiseq> asks the printer
    about itself:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>Faults</title>
        <p>What the printer is complaining of, such as the head open, paper
        or ribbon out, or the head too hot.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>What it is doing</title>
        <p>Whether it is paused, its buffer is full, labels are still to
        print, or a label is waiting to be taken from the peeler. A printer
        left in communications diagnostic mode is pointed out, since it then
        prints everything it is sent as a hex dump, which looks like a
        ruined label.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Memory</title>
        <p>RAM and Flash, each as a bar of what is used, with how much is
        free beside it: what decides whether another font or graphic will
        fit.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The printer itself</title>
        <p>Model, firmware, resolution, how long it has been on, and how far
        its head has printed, in the printer's own units.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <note>
      <p>A Zebra answers no ordinary status question while its paper or
      ribbon is out, its head is open, its rewinder is full or its head is
      too hot. LinuxZPL asks for the fault flags first, which a printer
      answers even then, and says when the rest went unanswered for that
      reason. A full rewinder raises no flag at all, and the window says
      that too.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Tick <gui>Auto-refresh</gui> to ask again every few seconds (2 to
    300; 5 to begin with) while you watch, or press <gui>Refresh</gui>.
    <gui>Copy</gui> puts the whole report on the clipboard, ready for a
    support request. If the printer stops answering, the last readings stay
    on screen rather than going blank.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="console">
    <title>The printer console</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>Printer</gui><gui>Console…</gui></guiseq> sends whatever
    you type to the printer, exactly as typed, and shows what it answers,
    or <gui>(no reply)</gui>. It is for commands nothing else here sends,
    such as a one-off setting or a question for the printer. It can be left
    open while you work on the label.</p>
  </section>
</page>
